{"id":"2610.31198","authors":[{"_id":"6adb372c121a70fc683456f0","name":"Ofek Avivi","hidden":false},{"_id":"6adb372c121a70fc683456f1","user":{"_id":"67b9e1ba9734000097f005e5","avatarUrl":"/avatars/5c9969abe1ae7818a30c3936e9c662e8.svg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Eliron Rahimi","user":"ElironRahimi","type":"user","name":"ElironRahimi"},"name":"Eliron Rahimi","status":"claimed_verified","statusLastChangedAt":"2026-10-23T16:45:04.599Z","hidden":false},{"_id":"6adb372c121a70fc683456f2","name":"Avi Mendelson","hidden":false},{"_id":"6adb372c121a70fc683456f3","name":"Chaim Baskin","hidden":false}],"publishedAt":"2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z","submittedOnDailyAt":"2026-10-23T00:00:00.000Z","title":"Different Tasks, Different Timesteps: Temporal Task-Aware Quantization for Diffusion Language Models","submittedOnDailyBy":{"_id":"67b9e1ba9734000097f005e5","avatarUrl":"/avatars/5c9969abe1ae7818a30c3936e9c662e8.svg","isPro":false,"fullname":"Eliron Rahimi","user":"ElironRahimi","type":"user","name":"ElironRahimi"},"summary":"Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) introduce unique quantization challenges due to their iterative denoising process. In this work, we reveal that quantization sensitivity is both taskand time-dependent, with different downstream tasks exhibiting distinct precision requirements across denoising steps. We further show that these patterns can be predicted without groundtruth labels or task-specific evaluation metrics. Building on these findings, we introduce T 2Quant, a task and time-aware mixedprecision quantization framework that uses a label-free sensitivity signal to optimally allocate activation precision across denoising steps under a fixed bit-width budget. Extensive experiments across three DLMs and six benchmarks demonstrate that T 2Quant consistently improves the average accuracy of existing quantization methods, achieving gains of up to 6.7 percentage points under the same budget.","upvotes":6,"discussionId":"6adb372d121a70fc683456f4","projectPage":"https: